<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ingrian Finns</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ingrians (Finnish), Inkerinsuomalaiset</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ingrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> are the Lutheran Finnish-speaking population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a>, around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a> and modern St Petersburg. They descend mainly from settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savonia">Savonia</a> and the Karelian Isthmus who moved in under Swedish rule in the 17th century, replacing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> inhabitants who left. Under Soviet rule they briefly formed the breakaway Republic of North Ingria in 1919–1920, and later suffered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> under Stalin; many emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> after 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ingrian-finns.jpg" alt="Ingrian Finns" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kasperi Hasala, Viron inkerinsuomalaisten liitto · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inkerin_lippu.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
